The energy has three parts, with the mass $m = 0.5\ \mathrm{kg}$ already given:

1. warm the ice from $-10\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ to $0\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$: $mc_{ice}\times10$ (needs the specific heat capacity of ice);
2. melt it at $0\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$: $mL_f$ (needs the specific latent heat of fusion);
3. warm the water from $0\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ to $20\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$: $mc_{water}\times20$ (needs the specific heat capacity of water).

The calorimeter is ignored, so its mass is not needed (B), the mass of water is the same $0.5\ \mathrm{kg}$ (A), and the time is irrelevant (D). **Answer: C.**
